## Lore

A combat soldier of the New German Republic with specialist training in communication, television, computers, sound equipment and surveillance. Beyond keeping information moving, the officer breaks codes, scrambles and unscrambles messages, and works in security, reconnaissance, espionage, sabotage of enemy computers and communication systems, and investigation.

Alignment: Any.

## Rank and pay

All communications officers start field duty at the rank of corporal. Salary is 1500 credits a month, rising to 3000 to 4000 for ninth level and higher, with quarters, food, clothing and medical care provided.

## Cybernetics

A cybernetic language translator, a universal headjack and an ear implant to start. Others may be purchased or awarded for acts of heroism and performance of duties.

## GM Notes

Issued alongside the stored equipment: a long-range directional radio and a radio scrambler. Neither is stated anywhere in the book''s equipment chapters, so neither is given a catalog row.

Equipment available upon assignment includes T-11 Enhanced body armour, the T-100 Eagle jet pack, a hover vehicle or other transport, additional weapons, cameras, surveillance and broadcasting equipment, and portable radar and sensors, plus medium-security access to most military bases, broadcasting stations, film and recording studios and editing facilities. Availability depends on the local commander, supply, location, casualties and combat conditions.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'ngr-communications-officer');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'ngr-communications-officer';
SELECT count(*) AS stub_gear FROM gear WHERE slug IN ('t-10-infantry-cyclops-body-armor', 'tx-42-laser-pulse-rifle');

